## 5. Backend Architecture Details

### 5.1 API surface

Core endpoints:
- `GET /healthz`
- `POST /api/v1/chat`
- `GET /api/v1/chat/presets`
- `GET /api/v1/auth/coros/login`
- `GET /api/v1/auth/coros/callback`
- `GET /api/v1/auth/coros/status`
- `POST /api/v1/auth/coros/logout`
- `POST /api/v1/activities/upload`
- `POST /api/v1/activities/analyze`

### 5.2 Session/auth model

OAuth state and sessions are MVP-grade.

Current stores:
- in-memory pending PKCE handshakes
- persisted COROS sessions in [api/data/coros_sessions.json](/home/ethan/work/projects/running-ai-coach/api/data/coros_sessions.json)

Implications:
- sessions survive server restart because they are written to disk
- this is still not a real multi-user persistence layer
- refresh tokens are not yet backed by a production-grade encrypted store

### 5.3 Shared domain model

The important contract is `ParsedFitActivity`, produced by both FIT parsing and COROS adaptation.

Downstream layers assume they receive:
- summary/session info
- lap list
- segment list
- metrics bundle

This keeps the rule layer independent from source-specific payload shapes.

### 5.4 Rule layer

The rule layer is in [analysis_engine.py](/home/ethan/work/projects/running-ai-coach/api/app/services/analysis_engine.py).

Current outputs include:
- `training_type`
- `intensity_level`
- `ef_ratio`
- `recovery_quality`
- `recovery_hr_drop_bpm`
- `fatigue_risk_level`
- `mechanics_stability_score`

Key characteristics:
- interval classification is structure-aware
- work segments are thresholded, not inferred from whole-run average pace alone
- EF ratio is treated as a trend/baseline signal, not a single-run quality score
- intensity is derived without requiring personal HR max configuration

### 5.5 Report layer

[report_builder.py](/home/ethan/work/projects/running-ai-coach/api/app/services/report_builder.py) converts analysis facts into deterministic Chinese output.

It produces:
- `verdict`
- `findings`
- `risks`
- `recommendation`

This is still rule-based, not LLM-generated.

### 5.6 Language layer

[coach_commentary.py](/home/ethan/work/projects/running-ai-coach/api/app/services/coach_commentary.py) is the only layer allowed to call the LLM.

It:
- builds a grounded prompt from facts
- includes optional COROS context and Level 1/2/3 adjustment flags
- returns `summary`, `strengths`, `watchouts`, `next_steps`
- falls back deterministically if the LLM fails

Important: the fallback path is a first-class runtime path, not a backup afterthought.

## 6. Frontend Architecture Details

The frontend is a Next.js App Router single page.

Current shape:
- OAuth gate if no COROS session
- chat view if connected
- preset messages for common coach actions
- message stream with optional embedded report card

State handled in the page component:
- COROS session id in `localStorage`
- connection status via `/api/v1/auth/coros/status`
- chat message list in component state

There is no global state library or client-side data cache yet.

## 7. External Dependencies / Integrations

Backend dependencies from [api/pyproject.toml](/home/ethan/work/projects/running-ai-coach/api/pyproject.toml):
- FastAPI
- fitdecode
- httpx
- pydantic v2
- python-multipart
- uvicorn

Frontend dependencies from [web/package.json](/home/ethan/work/projects/running-ai-coach/web/package.json):
- Next.js 16
- React 19
- Tailwind 4
- TypeScript
- ESLint

External services:
- COROS MCP at `https://mcpcn.coros.com/mcp`
- OpenAI-compatible LLM endpoint from `api/.env.local`

## 8. Runtime Configuration

Important environment-driven values:
- `LLM_API_KEY`
- `LLM_MODEL`
- `LLM_BASE_URL`
- `BACKEND_BASE_URL`
- `WEB_BASE_URL`
- `COROS_ACCESS_TOKEN` as fallback when no OAuth session is present

Potentially confusing but important:
- older docs and commands often use backend port `8000`
- current frontend and OAuth route defaults use backend port `8010`
  - [web/src/app/page.tsx](/home/ethan/work/projects/running-ai-coach/web/src/app/page.tsx) default API base is `http://127.0.0.1:8010`
  - [api/app/api/routes/auth.py](/home/ethan/work/projects/running-ai-coach/api/app/api/routes/auth.py) default backend base is `http://127.0.0.1:8010`

If an agent starts the backend on `8000` without overriding env vars, the current frontend/OAuth flow may not match it.

## 9. Storage and Persistence Reality

Current local storage inside the repo:
- [api/data/uploads](/home/ethan/work/projects/running-ai-coach/api/data/uploads): uploaded FIT files
- [api/data/coros_sessions.json](/home/ethan/work/projects/running-ai-coach/api/data/coros_sessions.json): persisted COROS sessions

What does not exist yet:
- no PostgreSQL or other real application database
- no formal user table
- no stored trend history beyond ad hoc local files
- no production-grade token storage

The future persistence direction is already designed in [docs/PERSISTENCE-DESIGN.md](/home/ethan/work/projects/running-ai-coach/docs/PERSISTENCE-DESIGN.md).

## 10. Known Architectural Constraints

1. The app is still effectively MVP-grade in persistence.
2. Chat intent routing is deterministic keyword logic, not an autonomous agent planner.
3. The frontend is intentionally thin; most business logic lives in backend services.
4. COROS is the primary product path; FIT upload is still available but secondary.
5. Some docs describe the original upload-first MVP and may lag behind the current chat-first UI.

